{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And Moses was frightened and said: Surely the thing is known.</b> The sin of speech — on account of which Israel remains in trouble more than all the other nations, for the sin of the evil tongue magnifies transgressions against idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and bloodshed. And the truth is not so, for on the contrary, Israel was redeemed by the merit that there was no evil tongue among you, and only these two were perpetrators of the evil tongue, and Scripture publicized them — a model of Shelomit daughter of Dibri (Leviticus 24:11), whom Scripture publicized."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The comment opens with a sharp, almost paradoxical transition from 'the sin of speech on account of which Israel remains in trouble more than all the nations' to 'and the truth is not so' — the adversative structure is preserved exactly as in the Hebrew. The reference to Israel being redeemed by the merit of freedom from lashon hara alludes to the midrashic tradition cited at Exodus Rabbah and parallels. 'These two' refers to the two Hebrew men who informed against Moses (Exodus 2:13)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:56.574779102Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}